# Choices that belong to the loopback application rather than to the codec
# driver, kept in a file of their own because two Kconfig roots need them: the
# sample's, and the native_sim test suite's - the suite compiles src/ of this
# sample, so it has to see the same symbols the sample was written against.
#
# Only what a human at a bench might have to change on the day is here.
# Everything else - the tone frequencies, the analyzer window, the pass/fail
# thresholds - is derived in src/loopback_format.h and src/loopback_verdict.h,
# because those numbers are tied to each other and to the levels in
# docs/hardware/akd4619-evaluation-board.md §4.5. A Kconfig symbol per threshold
# would let them be changed apart, which is the one thing that must not happen.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

choice
	prompt "I2S slot length on the wire"
	default AK4619_LOOPBACK_SLOT_32
	help
	  How many bit clocks one channel occupies. Both settings run MCLK at
	  256 fs, which is the ratio the AK4619's FS[2:0] = 000 row expects
	  (datasheet p.31, Table 1) and the only one this application uses: the
	  STM32 I2S driver compensates for its own master-clock divider, so
	  MCLK is 256 fs at either slot length and only BICK changes
	  (drivers/i2s/i2s_stm32.c at Zephyr v4.4.1 - `bit_clk_freq *=
	  channel_length == 16U ? 4U * 2U : 4U`).

	  The choice therefore is not about the clock ratio. It is about how
	  wide a word crosses the wire, and the two settings fail differently.

config AK4619_LOOPBACK_SLOT_32
	bool "32-bit slots, BICK 64 fs"
	help
	  The part's own default slot length, and the one that carries the
	  ADC's full 24-bit word: DODL has no 32-bit setting, so the ADC word
	  arrives MSB-justified in the slot with its low 8 bits zero
	  (datasheet p.33 Table 7, p.34 Figure 12).

	  KNOWN RISK, and the reason the other setting exists: Zephyr's STM32
	  I2S driver configures its DMA with a fixed 16-bit transfer width
	  whatever word_size says, so a 32-bit word reaches the peripheral as
	  two halves and the peripheral decides which half is the MSB. If that
	  order is the opposite of the little-endian one the wire seam writes,
	  the loop still runs at the right rate and still carries energy - what
	  comes back is the halves swapped, which the analyzer reports as noise
	  rather than as silence. See the sample's README.

config AK4619_LOOPBACK_SLOT_16
	bool "16-bit slots, BICK 32 fs"
	help
	  The fallback. 32 fs is the bottom of the BICK range FS[2:0] = 000
	  allows (32-256 fs, datasheet p.31), and 16-bit words are the depth
	  Zephyr's STM32 I2S driver moves with a DMA of matching width, so this
	  setting has no half-word order to get wrong. The cost is resolution:
	  the capture is 16-bit, about 96 dB of range instead of 144, which is
	  far more than any threshold in this application depends on.

	  Try this first if the 32-bit run reports energy without a tone.

endchoice
